WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
When applications call Ollama, they should treat it as a service boundary.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.000
It has request behavior, latency, errors, model availability, capacity limits,

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.000
and operational assumptions.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.000
Even if the service is running locally, it can still be unavailable, slow, overloaded,

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.000
misconfigured, or missing the model an application expects.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:21.000
API clients need timeouts, error handling, predictable model selection,

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.000
and clear assumptions about streaming or non-streaming behavior.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.100
A tool that works during a manual test may behave differently when multiple users are

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:30.280
active or when a model needs to load before responding.

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:34.520
Applications should avoid fragile assumptions about response timing, model availability,

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:36.000
or unlimited concurrency.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.890
Teams should decide how model names, prompts, options,

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:41.000
and user context are passed to the service.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.500
If a model name is hardcoded in several applications,

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:45.995
replacing or retiring that model becomes harder.

00:00:45.995 --> 00:00:48.180
If prompt options are scattered across clients,

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:50.995
support teams may not know which behavior users are seeing.

00:00:50.995 --> 00:00:55.000
A stable integration layer can make model selection and defaults easier to manage.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:58.371
Local APIs still need operational discipline when they support shared tools or business

00:00:58.371 --> 00:00:58.947
workflows.

00:00:58.947 --> 00:01:03.500
Clear integration boundaries help separate application bugs from runtime, model, storage,

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:05.000
and infrastructure issues.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:08.590
They also give operators a place to document expectations for timeouts, retries,

00:01:08.590 --> 00:01:11.280
request size, streaming, and failure behavior.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:15.000
Application teams should also decide what user context is appropriate to send.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:19.280
A local service can still receive sensitive prompts, file excerpts, or retrieved context.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:23.000
Integration code should keep data handling intentional, avoid unnecessary logging,

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:26.000
and make it clear which workflow owns each request.
